\description{
A frozen norm reference turns a raw construct score into a T score with
the calibration sample's mean and standard deviation. Those two constants
are estimates, so the T score a given raw score receives would differ
with another calibration sample. This function gives percentile
bootstrap intervals for the mean and the standard deviation of each
construct's calibration scores and for the T scores of three fixed raw
scores, so that the calibration's sampling uncertainty can be reported
beside the norms.
}
\details{
\strong{Targets.} For each construct: the calibration \code{mean} and \code{sd}, and
\code{T(x) = 50 + 10 * (x - m) / s} at three fixed raw scores \code{x}, the
calibration sample's 5th, 50th and 95th percentiles (type 7 quantiles,
held fixed across resamples; \code{fixed_score}), with \code{m} and \code{s} recomputed
in every resample.

\strong{Point values.} \code{estimate} is each target's value in the calibration
sample itself: the \code{mean} and \code{sd} of its scores, and for a fixed score
\code{50 + 10 * (fixed_score - mean) / sd} from them, so each interval stands
beside the value it is an interval for.

\strong{Method.} A percentile bootstrap (Efron & Tibshirani, 1993) of the
calibration rows, or of whole clusters when the reference was frozen
with cluster labels, so that the dependence within a cluster is kept;
\code{method}, \code{clusters} and \code{caution} say which unit was resampled and warn
when few clusters were. Each interval is pointwise (it is not a
simultaneous band over the three scores), and it describes the
calibration's sampling variability, not the measurement error in one
respondent's score. A limit is the type 7 quantile of the finite
resampled values (a resample whose SD is zero gives no T score). An
interval is withheld (\code{NA} limits) when fewer than 90 percent of the
resamples gave a finite value, or fewer than \code{2 / (1 - conf_level) - 1}
did (39 at 0.95; \code{successful} and \code{failed} count them). That count is
the floor SIEVER sets for every percentile interval. Its basis is the
order statistics: the smallest of \code{B} resamples cuts off, on average, a
share \code{1 / (B + 1)} of the bootstrap distribution, so with fewer than
\code{2 / (1 - conf_level) - 1} resamples every limit read from them cuts
off, on average, more than the tail of \code{(1 - conf_level) / 2} it stands
for. It is also the fewest at which boot's percentile rule, which places
a limit at position \code{(B + 1)(1 - conf_level) / 2} among the ordered
resamples, reaches the first of them. The type 7 limits used here lie
within the resamples at any count, so the floor is not a condition for
computing them; it is necessary, not sufficient, for a limit to reach
its tail. The intervals are approximate, with no coverage guarantee. The
number of resamples sets the Monte Carlo precision of the limits, not
what they estimate.

\strong{Seed.} With \code{seed = NULL} one integer is drawn from the session's
random stream and used; it is returned in \code{seed} (with \code{seed_supplied = FALSE}), so the run can be repeated exactly. The resampling runs in a
local stream (\code{withr::with_seed()}), so beyond that one draw the
session's random stream is left as it was.

\strong{Refusals} (an error): a reference that fails the plain-data check; a
clustered reference frozen without its cluster labels, or a reference of
repeated observations (use a longitudinal reference); a \code{conf_level}
outside (0, 1); and \code{B} that is not a whole number of at least 99 and at
least \code{2 / (1 - conf_level) - 1} (199 at 0.99).
}
